{{also|Winken}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|winken}}, from {{inh|de|goh|winken}}, from {{der|de|gem-pro|*winkijaną||to move side to side, sway}}, alternative form of {{m|gem-pro|*winkōną}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*weng-||to bow, bend, arch, curve}}. Compare {{m|de|wanken}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈvɪŋkŋ̩/|/ˈvɪŋkən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-winken.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-weak|class=3|winkt|winkte|[[gewunken]] or [[gewinkt]]}}

# to [[wave]]
# to [[beckon]]

====Usage notes====
* The originally weak verb started to develop alternative strong forms in Middle High German. These were long considered dialectal or colloquial, but the past participle {{m|de||gewunken}} has become standard and is increasingly replacing the now rarer {{m|de||gewinkt}}.<ref>{{cite-web
|date=March 14, 2017
|author=Jacqueline Kubczak
|author2=Matthias Mösch
|title=Gewinkt oder gewunken? — Schwache oder starke Flexion
|lang=de
|work=grammis 2.0
|url=http://hypermedia.ids-mannheim.de/call/public/fragen.ansicht?v_id=76
}}</ref><ref>{{R:GNV|gewinkt, gewunken|corpus=20}}</ref>

